"Natively Compatible Super-Efficient Lookup Arguments and How to Apply Them (https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/1058)\nciteturn3view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/1058\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 77\nL0: # What a lovely hat\nL1: #### Is it made out of 【0†tin foil†iacr.org】?\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Cryptology ePrint Archive】\nL4: \nL5:   * 【2†Papers】 Updates from the last:\nL6:     * 【3†7 days】\nL7:     * 【4†31 days】\nL8:     * 【5†6 months】\nL9:     * 【6†365 days】\nL10:     * * * *\nL11: \nL12:     * 【7†Listing by year】\nL13:     * 【8†All papers】\nL14:     * 【9†Compact view】\nL15:     * 【10†Subscribe†www.iacr.org】\nL16:     * * * *\nL17: \nL18:     * 【11†How to cite】\nL19:     * * * *\nL20:     * 【12†Harvesting metadata】\nL21:   * 【2†Submissions】\nL22:     * 【13†Submit a paper】\nL23:     * 【14†Revise or withdraw a paper】\nL24:     * 【15†Acceptance and publishing conditions】\nL25: \nL26: 【16†Image: Search Button】\nL27: \nL28: [Input] [Button: Search ]\nL29: #### Paper 2024/1058\nL30: \nL31: ### 【17†Natively Compatible Super-Efficient Lookup Arguments and How to Apply Them】\nL32: \nL33: Matteo Campanelli, Offchain Labs\nL34: \nL35: Dario Fiore, IMDEA Software\nL36: \nL37: Rosario Gennaro, The City College of New York\nL38: ##### Abstract\nL39: Lookup arguments allow an untrusted prover to commit to a vector $\\vec f \\in \\mathbb{F}^n$ and show that its entries reside in a predetermined table $\\vec t \\in \\mathbb{F}^N$. One of their key applications is to augment general-purpose SNARKs making them more efficient on subcomputations that are hard to arithmetize. In order for this \"augmentation\" to work out, a SNARK and a lookup argument should have some basic level of compatibility with respect to the commitment on $\\vec f$.\nL40: However, not all existing efficient lookup arguments are fully compatible with other efficient general-purpose SNARKs. This incompatibility can for example occur whenever SNARKs use multilinear extensions under the hood (e.g. Spartan) but the lookup argument is univariate in flavor (e.g. Caulk or $\\mathsf{cq}$).\nL41: In this paper we discuss how to widen the spectrum of \"super-efficient\" lookup arguments (where the proving time is independent of the size of the lookup table): we present a new construction inspired by $\\mathsf{cq}$and based on multilinear polynomial encodings (MLE). Our construction is the first lookup argument for any table that is also natively compatible with MLE-based SNARKs at comparable costs with other state-of-the-art lookup arguments, particularly when the large table is unstructured.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn3view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/succinctlabs/sp1/commit/d83ed91\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://github.com/succinctlabs/sp1/commit/d83ed91: Cache miss--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn3view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/compsec-epfl/efficient-sumcheck/blob/main/LICENSE-MIT\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://github.com/compsec-epfl/efficient-sumcheck/blob/main/LICENSE-MIT: Cache miss--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn3view3 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/compsec-epfl/efficient-sumcheck/blob/main/LICENSE-APACHE\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://github.com/compsec-epfl/efficient-sumcheck/blob/main/LICENSE-APACHE: Cache miss--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCommits · compsec-epfl/efficient-sumcheck · GitHub (https://github.com/compsec-epfl/efficient-sumcheck/commits/main/)\nciteturn3view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"re